  "assets": [
    {
      "assetId": "math.geom.midpoint-formula/learning_objective/1",
      "kind": "learning_objective",
      "conceptId": "math.geom.midpoint-formula",
      "title": "Learning Objectives",
      "content": "- LO1: Apply the midpoint formula $\\left(\\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}\\right)$ for the segment from $(x_1,y_1)$ to $(x_2,y_2)$ — AVERAGING the $x$-coordinates and, SEPARATELY, averaging the $y$-coordinates.\n- LO2: Recognize the formula is ORDER-INDEPENDENT — swapping which point is labeled $(x_1,y_1)$ versus $(x_2,y_2)$ gives the SAME midpoint, since addition (used in both averages) doesn't care about order.\n- LO3: Use the midpoint formula IN REVERSE — given one endpoint and the midpoint, find the OTHER endpoint (by solving the averaging equation for the unknown coordinate).",
      "localKey": null,
      "language": "en",
      "refs": [],
